{"name":"French 75","hex":"#f9f3d5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"French 75","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The Canon de 75 modèle 1897, an artillery piece capable of firing 75 mm shells.","A cocktail made with gin, champagne, and lemon juice."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/French_75"}],"entries":[{"title":"Canon de 75 modèle 1897","blocks":[{"text":"The French 75 mm field gun is a quick-firing field artillery piece adopted in March 1898. Its official French designation was: Canon de 75 mm Mle 1897. It was commonly known as the French 75, simply the 75 and Soixante-Quinze. The French 75 was designed as an anti-personnel weapon system for delivering large volumes of time-fused shrapnel shells on enemy troops advancing in the open. After 1915 and the onset of trench warfare, impact-detonated high-explosive shells prevailed. By 1918, the 75 became the main agents of delivery for toxic gas shells. The 75s also became widely used as truck mounted anti-aircraft artillery. They were the main armament of the Saint-Chamond tank in 1918 and the Char 2C."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/0_Canon_de_75_mm_mod%C3%A8le_1897_-_Mus%C3%A9e_de_l%27arm%C3%A9e_%C3%A0_Paris_2.JPG/330px-0_Canon_de_75_mm_mod%C3%A8le_1897_-_Mus%C3%A9e_de_l%27arm%C3%A9e_%C3%A0_Paris_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Canon_de_75_mod%C3%A8le_1897","wikidata":"Q958738"},{"title":"French 75 (cocktail)","blocks":[{"text":"French 75 is a cocktail made from gin, champagne, lemon juice, and sugar. It is also called a 75 cocktail, or in French simply a soixante quinze."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/French_75.jpg/330px-French_75.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_75_(cocktail)","wikidata":"Q3753012"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https